1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,918
Orchard Core can let people sign in with their Google Account.

2
00:00:04,918 --> 00:00:09,470
The Google Authentication feature adds a Google button to the login page.

3
00:00:09,470 --> 00:00:14,943
This video covers enabling the feature, entering the credentials from Google, and finding

4
00:00:14,943 --> 00:00:16,960
the button on the login page.

5
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:26,989
With Google Authentication, the login page shows a Google button next to the login form.

6
00:00:26,989 --> 00:00:30,859
A person selects it, signs in at Google, and approves access.

7
00:00:30,859 --> 00:00:33,920
Google then sends the person back to your site.

8
00:00:34,670 --> 00:00:37,447
Google needs to know about your site first.

9
00:00:37,447 --> 00:00:42,263
In the Google API console, you create credentials for your application, and you

10
00:00:42,263 --> 00:00:44,643
get a Client ID and a client secret.

11
00:00:44,643 --> 00:00:48,950
You also register an authorized redirect URI that points to your site.

12
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:54,156
When Google sends the person back, Orchard Core signs in the user linked

13
00:00:54,156 --> 00:00:55,812
to that Google Account.

14
00:00:55,812 --> 00:00:59,982
If a user has the same email address, the person links the accounts

15
00:00:59,982 --> 00:01:01,810
with that user's password.

16
00:01:01,810 --> 00:01:06,380
Otherwise, the person registers, unless external registration is disabled.

17
00:01:10,583 --> 00:01:13,212
The feature is part of the Google module.

18
00:01:13,212 --> 00:01:16,343
Let's open Tools, Features, and search for Google.

19
00:01:16,801 --> 00:01:22,394
Google Analytics, Google Authentication, and Google Tag Manager share the Google category.

20
00:01:22,394 --> 00:01:29,211
Google Authentication authenticates users with their Google Account, and it depends on External Authentication.

21
00:01:29,661 --> 00:01:32,733
Let's select Enable on Google Authentication.

22
00:01:32,733 --> 00:01:39,481
External Authentication is enabled with it, and a message confirms that Google Authentication was enabled.

23
00:01:43,710 --> 00:01:47,672
Google Authentication adds an entry to the admin menu:

24
00:01:47,672 --> 00:01:50,909
Settings, Security, Authentication, Google.

25
00:01:50,909 --> 00:01:52,230
Let's open it.

26
00:01:52,687 --> 00:01:56,961
A warning says the website might be restarted when you save the settings,

27
00:01:56,961 --> 00:01:59,665
which can make it unresponsive for a moment.

28
00:01:59,665 --> 00:02:01,667
A link opens the documentation.

29
00:02:02,124 --> 00:02:06,745
Under Google Authentication Settings, Client ID is marked as required.

30
00:02:06,745 --> 00:02:11,124
Let's type the client ID that Google created for your application.

31
00:02:11,686 --> 00:02:14,779
API secret key takes the client secret from Google.

32
00:02:14,779 --> 00:02:16,298
It's a password field.

33
00:02:16,298 --> 00:02:19,986
What you type is stored encrypted, and it's never shown again.

34
00:02:20,450 --> 00:02:24,694
CallbackPath is the path where Google returns the person to your site.

35
00:02:24,694 --> 00:02:29,217
Leave it empty to use the default, signin-google, which is its placeholder.

36
00:02:29,217 --> 00:02:33,070
The redirect URI you register in Google must use the same path.

37
00:02:33,524 --> 00:02:37,551
Store tokens keeps the external authentication tokens for reuse.

38
00:02:37,551 --> 00:02:40,944
It's cleared by default, and let's leave it that way.

39
00:02:41,416 --> 00:02:42,746
Let's select Save.

40
00:02:42,746 --> 00:02:47,180
A message confirms that the site settings were updated, and the API secret

41
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:48,732
key field stays empty.

42
00:02:48,732 --> 00:02:51,836
Its placeholder says the value is securely stored.

43
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:57,916
Let's open the login page.

44
00:02:57,916 --> 00:03:02,018
Next to the Log in form, a section titled Use another service to

45
00:03:02,018 --> 00:03:04,420
log in lists the external services.

46
00:03:04,872 --> 00:03:07,609
It holds one button, labeled Google.

47
00:03:07,609 --> 00:03:13,082
The button appears only when a Client ID and an API secret key are both saved.

48
00:03:13,544 --> 00:03:19,520
Selecting Google sends the browser to Google, where the person signs in and approves access.

49
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:23,484
The credentials in this video are fake, so let's stop here.

50
00:03:27,684 --> 00:03:30,007
Two more things apply to this page.

51
00:03:30,007 --> 00:03:35,050
The Administrator role has the permission to manage Google Authentication settings.

52
00:03:35,050 --> 00:03:39,244
And when the Secrets feature is enabled, the API secret key can come

53
00:03:39,244 --> 00:03:41,794
from a secret instead of a typed value.

54
00:03:42,544 --> 00:03:47,188
The External Login tab, in the User Login settings, can send people straight

55
00:03:47,188 --> 00:03:49,681
to Google when it is the only service.

56
00:03:49,681 --> 00:03:56,421
The External Authentication tab, in the Registration settings, controls registration through an external service.

57
00:03:56,421 --> 00:03:58,404
A separate video covers both.

58
00:04:00,404 --> 00:04:05,576
To let people sign in with Google, enable Google Authentication, enter the Client

59
00:04:05,576 --> 00:04:08,548
ID and API secret key from Google, and save.

60
00:04:08,548 --> 00:04:11,818
The Google button then appears on the login page.

61
00:04:11,818 --> 00:04:13,364
Thanks for watching.

